@ 37.2.3 - Proto-Siouan to Mandan
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan I”. International Journal of American Linguistics 16(2):61 – 66; Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan II” International Journal of American Linguistics 16(3):113 – 121; Wolff, Hans (1950), “Comparative Siouan III”. International Journal of American Linguistics 16(4):168 – 178; and cedh audmanh’s changes above, which assisted me in deciphering the vintage phonetic transcription scheme NB: Does not include developments in unstressed non-nasal vowels. L was apparently either /ɹ/ or /l/; Lʲ (Lʸ in the text) was apparently /j/, or maybe /ʎ/. “Phonemic vowel length was gained somehow.”
	s > ʃ
	t > ∅ / _s
	ç > x
	w > m
	Lʲ > n / _V:[+nas]
	L > r / _V:[-nas]
	ã > a / in % (sporadic)
	tʲ > s / _ʔV
	CʔV > CVʔV
	tʲr > sVrV
	r > ∅ / k_
	mn > mVnV / #_
	mn > mVnV / :{ {C,V}_C, {C,V}_V }:
	sn > ʃVnV, sVrV
	km > kVpV
	t > ∅ / _k, when medial
	sk > ʃ / _V